     Dave Hairston recently mentioned that Excalibur uses up 
food.  Does it really?  I used wizard mode to check and, at 
least when standing still, it didn't seem to do a thing.  Any 
of its special functions might consume food, although I don't 
see why regenerative ability need do so (a ring of 
regeneration does its 3/2 or 4/3 or whatever in gethungry(), 
and no special weapons are referenced there).
     (This is assuming that Excalibur, as I think was implied, 
gives regeneration, which I didn't know previously if it's 
true.)
     In any case, I ate a black dragon recently (Greg's 
managed to eek out a good game again-- +5 Excalibur, +3 
Stormy, AC -17, level 15) and got no particular message when I 
finished.  Can I still potentially have disintegration 
resistance?  I'd *really* love to have it, since that would 
top off the list of resistances (I found, to my surprise, 
after quaffing a blessed potion of enlightenment that I have 
fire resistance now, somehow, in addition to all the others) 
and remove the greatest threat to my game.
 
     I've always been interested in knowing just how common 
the HARD option really is.  I hear about some people "spending 
weeks" casting create monster and killing off the monster-- I 
tried it with a 10th level wizard or so in wizard mode and it 
only gave me 10-15 casts before the spell was gone.  On my 
Unix box, no one's even topped half a million points (I've done 
slightly better than the top score, last I checked, on my PC, 
still not quite topping 500K, and my present game is 
approaching, if not all that rapidly.  I spent half a day or so 
moving all the objects from levels 10-17 up to level 9 and 
then moving the objects from levels 1-5 as far down as level 
6, at which point I figured it was best not to count one's 
eggs until they're hatched and wait until I actually have a 
wand of polymorph before finishing setting up my polypiles).
From what I've seen of the code, the HARD option is quite a 
pain.  (Or, rather, the less-than-hard option is not).  For 
instance, you actually get a warning before the gods decide 
whether or not they're going to take away an experience level 
(although, as far as I remember, u.ugangr still goes positive, 
so you have to mollify the gods).  And you don't have to wait 
an extra 1000 turns or so between prayers if you have H of E.
     I also note that being chaotic is a mixed blessing when 
it comes to sacrificing.  +40% over neutrals and lawfuls for 
sacrifice value, but you have to find a monster 150% as big 
and mean in order to mollify your gods.  (Should it really be 
a one-shot deal?  I'd think sacrificing 35 fire giants would 
do the trick as well as one titan, albeit the former might not 
work.  Perhaps u.ugangr should be a float (or at least a 
larger integer, say, in units of 10 or so) and smaller 
monsters should be able to decrease it smaller amounts, although
they'd still have to be a certain value.
 
     My favorite "theoretical" (==unlikely to execute) part of 
the code would have to be handling sacrificing the amulet at 
the wrong altar.  You get an "escaped with the amulet" message 
rather than an "ascended..." status, but the interaction is 
rather funny, esp. with the code comments ("/* and the 
opposing team picks him up and carries him off on their 
shoulders */")
 
